why 2 0 SIX_FOUR This works like a cadential ⁶₄ in D major: the D chord over A resolves onto the A chord on the next beat. For a moment the music is in D, the relative major.
why 3 1/8 SPACING The bass holds a low A while the tenor climbs E–F♯–G; the gap is wide for one eighth.
why 4 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION A cadence in B minor where every resolution falls: soprano C♯–B, the alto's A♯ drops to F♯, the seventh E to D and the bass to B.
why 8 0 LEAPS The bass climbs G♯–A♯–B–C♯–D by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 8 1/4 LEAPS The alto climbs D–E–F♯–G♯ by step. A scale line hides the tritone between its ends.
why 8 1/2 SIMILAR_MOTION A half cadence on F♯: the alto's E♯ rises to F♯, the tenor G♯–A♯, the tune B–C♯ and the bass leaps up to F♯. Every line rises into the dominant.
why 11 0 DOUBLING Soprano (D–C♯) and tenor (B–C♯) meet on C♯ by step from opposite sides.
why 11 3/4 DOUBLING The ⁶₄ starts the final cadence. Soprano and tenor both hold D, the soprano about to fall to C♯ and the tenor to G♯; the doubling follows their lines.
why 12 0 SPACING The tenor drops to G♯ for the V of V chord while the alto holds a high B. The gap lasts one beat; the tenor rises to C♯ next.
why 12 1/4 LEAPS After its leap down the tenor turns back up by a fourth to C♯, inside the leap, instead of a step. C♯ is the fifth the dominant chord needs.
